  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As of 2:35 AM EDT...Current GOES 16 WV imagery \nshows a closed upper low (ULL) sitting near Nova Scotia, with a \nshortwave disturbance diving south into upstate NY around its \nwestern periphery. While there isn't a ton of moisture, this has\nstill provided enough lift for a few showers across western New\nEngland, although current radar trends suggest the bulk of this\nactivity will remain mainly east of our area through the rest \nof tonight into this morning. With mostly cloudy conditions, \nlows will generally be in the 50s to low 60s. \n\nToday through Tuesday, sfc high pressure builds down from the north \nbeneath amplifying ridging aloft. Large-scale subsidence will\nresult in mainly dry conditions for this time period. Today \nwill feature below normal temps (60s to low 70s), but \ntemperatures trend closer to normal tomorrow and Tuesday for \ndaytime highs. Overnight lows will be seasonably cool (40s to \n50s) each night with favorable radiational cooling conditions \nunder the sfc high. Patchy fog will also be possible each of the\nnext few nights. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Wednesday into Thursday, a warm front attempts to \nlift northwards into the region as a potent sfc low passes well to \nour north near James Bay. While the best forcing should remain well \nto our north, a pre-frontal disturbance Wednesday afternoon or \nevening will likely lead to some scattered showers, especially for \nnorthern areas. Guidance is in fairly good agreement that \ninstability will be very limited due in part to meager mid-level \nlapse rates. So, while a few rumbles of thunder may be possible, the \nprobability for severe weather is very low. While WPC has our \nnorthern areas in a marginal risk ERO, this seems to represent a \nworst-case scenario, as flooding appears unlikely given \nantecedent dry conditions, lack of instability, and fast storm \nmotions. We collaborated with BTV and GYX on the messaging, \nwhich is that, by and large, any rainfall received will be \nbeneficial. Some showers may continue into Thursday as the cold \nfront will be slow to drop south of our region, but as it \nfinally does so tranquil weather returns for the end of the week\nwith another area of high pressure building overhead. \nTemperatures also look to trend cooler Friday into next weekend \nbehind the cold front.",
